WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.134 --> 00:00:18.513
A few days after the gathering at Ruby's, Anne and Diana were walking slowly near the stream next to Green Gables.

2
00:00:18.513 --> 00:00:21.320
"What are you thinking, Anne?" asked Diana.

3
00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:25.913
"I've been thinking about writing a short story," Anne confessed.

4
00:00:25.913 --> 00:00:30.388
"I'm hoping I can get it published, in a women's magazine maybe."

5
00:00:30.388 --> 00:00:35.733
"Oh, have you started it?" exclaimed Diana, very interested.

6
00:00:35.733 --> 00:00:41.836
"I've only written a few pages, but I've got a suitable plot," Anne replied.

7
00:00:41.836 --> 00:00:48.416
"The main character's name is Averil. The hero's name is Harold Jonathon Wittington.

8
00:00:48.416 --> 00:00:52.855
I think they are elegant and fitting names for the main characters, don't you?

9
00:00:52.855 --> 00:00:56.301
It took me hours to think of those names, Diana."

10
00:00:56.301 --> 00:01:00.511
"Have you named all the characters?" asked Diana wistfully.

11
00:01:00.511 --> 00:01:07.432
"If you haven't, could I name someone? Even if it's just some unimportant person."

12
00:01:07.432 --> 00:01:11.106
"You can name the servant," conceded Anne.

13
00:01:11.106 --> 00:01:16.176
"I think his name will be . . . Bobby," said Diana promptly.

14
00:01:16.176 --> 00:01:19.111
"And you'll let me read your story, won't you, Anne?"

15
00:01:19.111 --> 00:01:27.568
"When it is finished," Anne said, "I'll read it to you and Mr. Harrison." He was Anne's neighbor in Avonlea.

16
00:01:27.568 --> 00:01:35.651
"And I want you both to criticize it severely, since no one else shall see it until it is published."

17
00:01:35.651 --> 00:01:41.985
"How are you going to end the story? Happily or unhappily?" Diana asked.

18
00:01:41.985 --> 00:01:50.461
"I'm not sure," Anne said. "I'd like it to end unhappily, because that would be so much more romantic.

19
00:01:50.461 --> 00:01:55.645
But I think magazine editors have a prejudice against sad endings.

20
00:01:55.645 --> 00:02:06.622
A famous author once said that only a genius should write an unhappy ending. And I am anything but a genius."

21
00:02:06.622 --> 00:02:09.569
"Oh, I like happy endings best.

22
00:02:09.569 --> 00:02:19.320
I think the hero should marry Averil," said Diana, who, especially since her engagement to Fred, thought this was how every story should end.

23
00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:23.595
"But don't you like to cry when you read stories?" Anne asked.

24
00:02:23.595 --> 00:02:33.083
"Yes," Diana said. "But I only like crying in the middle of the story. I like everything to end happily."

25
00:02:33.083 --> 00:02:39.390
"Hmm, perhaps I should have one tragic scene," said Anne thoughtfully.

26
00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:46.686
"Perhaps Bobby the servant is injured in a tractor accident in the fields and dies—"

27
00:02:46.686 --> 00:02:51.844
"No! You can't kill Bobby!" said Diana, laughing.

28
00:02:51.844 --> 00:02:57.340
"He belongs to me and I want him to live and flourish! Kill someone else."

29
00:02:57.340 --> 00:03:02.776
"But it's my story!" Anne laughed with her.

30
00:03:02.776 --> 00:03:08.104
Over the next fortnight, Anne worked hard on her story, writing day and night.

31
00:03:08.104 --> 00:03:13.181
She managed to include a tragic scene without killing Bobby the servant.

32
00:03:13.181 --> 00:03:18.674
When the story was finally finished, Anne read it to Diana on the porch of Green Gables.

33
00:03:18.674 --> 00:03:25.162
When Anne looked up after reading the last paragraph, Diana seemed disappointed.

34
00:03:25.162 --> 00:03:29.585
"Why did you kill Anthony Lennox?" Diana asked reproachfully.

35
00:03:29.585 --> 00:03:35.099
"He was the villain," protested Anne. "He had to be punished!"

36
00:03:35.099 --> 00:03:40.475
"I liked him best of all!" said Diana, unreasonably, it seemed to Anne.

37
00:03:40.475 --> 00:03:45.988
"Well, he's dead, and he'll have to stay dead," said Anne, annoyed.

38
00:03:45.988 --> 00:03:54.873
"If I had let him live, he would have kept persecuting Averil and Harold. The villain had to die, Diana!"

39
00:03:54.873 --> 00:04:03.119
"Well, anyway," Diana said, "it's a wonderful story, Anne, and it will make you famous, I'm sure. What's its title?"

40
00:04:03.119 --> 00:04:10.474
"Oh, I decided that long ago," Anne replied. "It's called 'Averil's Atonement.'

41
00:04:10.474 --> 00:04:15.050
Both words begin with the letter a, so it sounds sophisticated.

42
00:04:15.050 --> 00:04:21.268
Now, Diana, tell me honestly, do you see any faults in my story?"

43
00:04:21.268 --> 00:04:31.233
"Well," hesitated Diana, "that part where Averil makes the cake doesn't seem quite romantic enough to match the rest of the story.

44
00:04:31.233 --> 00:04:33.315
Heroines shouldn't cook."

45
00:04:33.315 --> 00:04:38.923
"But that's a funny part, and one of the best parts of the story!" Anne exclaimed.

46
00:04:38.923 --> 00:04:46.909
Diana prudently refrained from any further criticism, but later Mr. Harrison criticized the story a lot.

47
00:04:46.909 --> 00:04:52.393
"Why did you write a story about rich people with silly names?" he asked.

48
00:04:52.393 --> 00:04:56.289
"You should write about the people you know, here in Avonlea."

49
00:04:56.289 --> 00:05:00.794
"But Avonlea is not romantic," Anne protested.

50
00:05:00.794 --> 00:05:06.400
"Well," Mr. Harrison replied, "I don't like the hero, Harold, either.

51
00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:16.101
He's too good. He just stands around being polite and romantic. I prefer the villain, Anthony Lennox."

52
00:05:16.101 --> 00:05:21.255
Anne decided the next time she wrote a story, she wouldn't ask for anyone's feedback!

53
00:05:21.255 --> 00:05:28.561
Anne sent the story to one of the biggest women's magazines and waited to hear from them. She didn't have to wait long.

54
00:05:28.561 --> 00:05:36.802
The magazine sent the story back a week later with a letter that said "Averil's Atonement" wasn't "suitable."

55
00:05:36.802 --> 00:05:43.752
Diana tried to comfort Anne, saying, "Those big magazines are no good anyway," and that she should keep trying.

56
00:05:43.752 --> 00:05:48.225
Not to be dissuaded, Anne sent the story to another magazine.

57
00:05:48.225 --> 00:05:53.744
It was sent back even faster. Anne didn't believe the editor had read it at all!

58
00:05:53.744 --> 00:06:00.953
Discouraged, she put her story away underneath her dresser and gave the extra copy to Diana.

59
00:06:00.953 --> 00:06:08.464
Anne never mentioned the two rejections to Mr. Harrison, but one evening he asked her bluntly if her story had been accepted.

60
00:06:08.464 --> 00:06:13.129
"No, the editors didn't accept it," Anne answered briefly.

61
00:06:13.129 --> 00:06:16.352
Mr. Harrison looked at Anne's flushed face.

62
00:06:16.352 --> 00:06:20.968
"Well, I hope you keep writing, Anne," he said encouragingly.

63
00:06:20.968 --> 00:06:26.417
"No, I shall never try to write a story again," declared Anne.

64
00:06:26.417 --> 00:06:32.856
"I wouldn't give up altogether," said Mr. Harrison gently, looking at Anne's flushed face.

65
00:06:32.856 --> 00:06:35.524
"I would keep writing once in a while.

66
00:06:35.524 --> 00:06:38.933
Why don't you write about people and places you know?

67
00:06:38.933 --> 00:06:47.714
Make the characters talk in everyday English, and write about everyday life. But don't stop writing, Anne."

68
00:06:47.714 --> 00:06:52.463
"No. It was foolish of me to try," Anne said firmly.

69
00:06:52.463 --> 00:06:57.305
"I am good at teaching, and when I finish college, I'll return to that.

70
00:06:57.305 --> 00:07:01.074
I can teach. I can't write stories."

71
00:07:01.074 --> 00:07:06.177
"When you finish college, it'll be time to get married, Anne," said Mr. Harrison.

72
00:07:06.177 --> 00:07:19.049
Anne scowled. Sometimes Mr. Harrison was so annoying! She got up and marched home.
